<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castillo San Felipe del Morro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1539">1539</a>–1790s</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castillo San Felipe del Morro is a Spanish citadel on the headland guarding the entrance to San Juan Bay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>. Begun in 1539 and expanded over two and a half centuries into a massive stone fortress, it withstood English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> attacks, though the Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a>'s forces took it in 1598. It is part of the San Juan National Historic Site and a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castillo-san-felipe-del-morro.jpg" alt="Castillo San Felipe del Morro" width="320" height="135" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chad Sparkes from Kissimmee,Florida, United States · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo_San_Felipe_Del_Morro_(16869927311).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
